# Cyclistic Bike-share Analysis  

## Background

Cyclistic, a bike sharing company centralized in Chicago featuring over 5800
bicycles and 600 docking stations, has decided to maximize their customer
subscription rate to ensure their future success. With a large variety of
options for people with disabilities and riders who can't use traditional
bikes, they have garnered a large number of leisurely riders while ensuring
they have a stable base of users biking to their work and back.  

Lily Moreno, the director of marketing at Cyclistic, determined that the best
way to ensure its future success is to increase their number of annual
memberships. As such, this analysis will look into the usage of bikes and
see how they differ between existing subscribers and casual riders, deciding
whether or not a new marketing strategy to increase membership rates by
targeting casual riders will lead to future success.  


## Ask  

Throughout this case study, the question being answered is as follows:
"How do annual members and casual riders use Cyclistic bikes differently?"  

This question prompts us to look at the riding habits of annual members and
casual riders and see what is different between them. We will see what is the
best path to expanding the amount of subscribers by seeing what is popular
among the users, who will be more inclined to become a subscriber, and how to
best market the subscription service to get maximum retention among the most
likely candidates.  


## Prepare

In preparing for analysis two data sets were given from Cyclistic's public data
and were used for the analysis. Both data sets have values for each instance
a bike was used, with a duration, a starting station, and an end station, as
well as whether the person using the bike is a subscriber or a casual user. The
2019 dataset also has information on the gender and birth year of the users,
while the 2020 dataset has information on the longitude and latitude of the
starting and ending stations.

From all of this information, we will use the station names, the ride durations,
and the age/gender from the 2019 dataset to conduct analysis on. The rest of the
data will not be used in this analysis since it doesn't relate to the objective
of this data analysis, and will not provide any beneficial insight to the
stakeholders regarding boosting subscription rates.


## Process

For this analysis, the two datasets were processed using Google Sheets and
its data-cleaning functionality. The first step was to use the trim whitespace
and remove duplicates functions on the entire dataset to check for those in
the data. After this was done, the data was then filtered and searched to 
locate any empty values which were then removed from the data, which removed
18,022 rows between both datasets. Once this was done, columns that had 
numeric values were ensured to have correct types, and any misspelled values
were checked using pivot tables. 

The 2020 data was lacking over half of the trip duration data, which was easily
calculated and inserted into all values. Other than that, no other data had to
be modified in order to ensure the veracity of the data.

The integrity of the data was maintained during this cleaning process as all
of this was done to a copy of the data, while Cyclistic's original dataset 
remained unmodified. On top of this, every step in the cleaning process was
logged and kept track of. As such, the data is ready to analyze.


## Analysis and Share

In order to perform the analysis on the cleaned datasets, they needed to be
modified first to show the ride length and day of the week for each ride. 
As such, here is how they were loaded and mutated:

```{r load}
# Load 2019 Data
df_2019 <- read_csv("Divvy_Trips_2019_Q1.csv") %>% 
  clean_names() %>%
  mutate(
    start_time = mdy_hms(start_time),
    end_time = mdy_hms(end_time),
    ride_length = as.numeric(difftime(end_time, start_time, units = "mins")),
    day_of_week = wday(start_time, label = TRUE),
    member_casual = if_else(usertype == "Subscriber", "member", "casual"),
    age = 2019 - birthyear
  ) %>% 
  rename(started_at = start_time, ended_at = end_time) %>%
  filter(ride_length > 0, !is.na(gender), !is.na(birthyear), birthyear > 1920 & birthyear < 2010)

# Load 2020 Data
df_2020 <- read_csv("Divvy_Trips_2020_Q1.csv") %>% 
  clean_names() %>%
  mutate(
    started_at = ymd_hms(started_at),
    ended_at = ymd_hms(ended_at),
    ride_length = as.numeric(difftime(ended_at, started_at, units = "mins")),
    day_of_week = wday(started_at, label = TRUE)
  ) %>% 
  filter(ride_length > 0)
```

Once the datasets were given the extra columns, they needed to be combined as
shown here:

```{r combine}
all_trips <- bind_rows(
  df_2020 %>% dplyr::select(started_at, ended_at, ride_length, day_of_week, member_casual),
  df_2019 %>% dplyr::select(started_at, ended_at, ride_length, day_of_week, member_casual)
)
```

Once the data was combined and consolidated into the "all_trips" table, it
became ready to analyze on how the differences between the casual riders and
the subscribers came into play. 


### Ride Length Overview

Showcased here is how the ride lengths were analyzed in order to see the
average, median, and standard deviation for all rides, grouping all casual
or subscribing riders together to highlight their totals.

```{r summaries, echo=FALSE}
all_trips %>%
  group_by(member_casual) %>%
  summarise(
    avg_duration = mean(ride_length),
    median_duration = median(ride_length),
    sd_duration = sd(ride_length),
    total_rides = n()
  ) |> 
  knitr::kable()
```

From this, one can see how the average duration of trips hovers at around 90
minutes for casual members, while subscribers' average is much lower at 13
minutes. Given that the standard deviation for casual riders is exceptionally
large at over 1600 minutes, the data spreads greatly, which means the average
could be skewed, which is consistent with the median being 21 minutes. Because
of this, there are a large section of casual users who bike for shorter times
with a smaller portion biking for an extended duration.

While this is the case for casual users, subscribers have a lot more consistent
usage, as shown with the lower standard deviation on the much larger section
of data. Since this is the case, and the average is significantly lower for
subscribers, there could be an advertisement campaign to target any casual
users who are using bikes for a shorter duration since they match the habits
of subscribers.


### Rides by Weekday (With Counts)

In order to see the changes from one day to another, two columns were created,
showcasing the percent of all rides in data (split between casual riders and
subscribers) for a particular weekday. As shown below, this division was
shown in the column chart, showcasing how the number of rides change based
on the day of the week.

```{r weekdays, echo=FALSE, message=FALSE}
weekday_data <- all_trips %>%
  group_by(member_casual, day_of_week) %>%
  summarise(n = n()) %>%
  group_by(member_casual) %>%
  mutate(percent = n / sum(n), label = paste0(n, " (", percent(percent, accuracy = 0.1), ")"))

ggplot(weekday_data, aes(x = day_of_week, y = n, fill = member_casual)) +
  geom_col(position = position_dodge(), show.legend = FALSE) +
  geom_text(aes(label = label), position = position_dodge(width = 0.9), vjust = 0.5, size = 2.5, angle = 90) +
  labs(title = "Rides by Day of Week", x = "Day", y = "Total Rides") +
  theme_minimal() +
  facet_wrap(~member_casual)
```

Based on this chart, one can deduce that members use the bike share mostly on
weekdays, while almost half of all casual users ride bikes during the weekends.
Looking at this one can infer that using some form of advertising during the
weekends for showcasing the benefits of membership to casual users would
be the most effective in garnering new members.


### Station Popularity Analysis

Cyclistic operates numerous bike-sharing stations throughout Chicago. 
Understanding which stations are most frequently used allows the marketing 
team to identify high-traffic areas for targeted campaigns. Below are 
insights into the most popular start and end stations, 
as well as the most common trip connections. 
 
```{r start_stations_display, echo=FALSE, message=FALSE, results='hide'}
stations <- bind_rows(
  df_2019 %>% mutate(
    start_station_name = from_station_name,
    end_station_name = to_station_name
  ),
  df_2020 )

```


```{r station_plot_display, echo=FALSE, message=FALSE}
# Get top start stations
top_start <- stations %>%
  count(station_name = start_station_name, sort = TRUE) %>%
  top_n(10, n) %>%
  mutate(type = "Start")

# Get top end stations
top_end <- stations %>%
  count(station_name = end_station_name, sort = TRUE) %>%
  top_n(10, n) %>%
  mutate(type = "End")

# Combine and compute label
top_combined <- bind_rows(top_start, top_end) %>%
  mutate(type = factor(type, levels = c("Start", "End"))) %>% 
  group_by(type) %>%
  mutate(
    percentage = round(n / sum(n) * 100, 1),
    label = paste0(n, " (", percentage, "%)")
  )

# Plot: clear labels inside bars
ggplot(top_combined, aes(x = reorder(station_name, n), y = n, fill = type)) +
  geom_bar(stat = "identity") +
  geom_text(
    aes(label = label),
    hjust = 1.05,               # Puts label inside bar
    color = "white",
    size = 3.5
  ) +
  coord_flip() +
  facet_wrap(~type, scales = "free_y") +
  labs(
    title = "Top 10 Most Popular Stations",
    x = "Station Name",
    y = "Number of Rides"
  ) +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme(
    strip.text = element_text(face = "bold", size = 11),
    plot.title = element_text(face = "bold", size = 14),
    legend.position = "none"
  )


```

The top 10 most popular starting and ending Stations represent the most frequent 
origins and  likely to finish for rides, suggesting high 
foot traffic or strategic locations like downtown hubs or transit intersections. 

The graphs show that the top four stations for both starting and ending stations 
are the same, revealing where riders are likely to start and finish 
trips—often are near business districts, residential areas, or recreation zones.

#### Top 10 Most Common Station Pairs (Connections)
```{r plot_network_pair_station, echo=FALSE, message=FALSE}
