  theme(plot.title = element_text(face = "bold", size = 14, hjust = 0.5))


```


The top connections reflect popular commuting or leisure routes, which could 
benefit from promotional partnerships or added infrastructure. Starting stations 
and ending stations often overlap but are not always the same, suggesting 
asymmetry in travel patterns.

As such, top station pairs can indicate common commuter routes, especially if 
occurring consistently across both years. Columbus Dr & Randolph St is the fifth 
starting station but it is not in the top 10 of the ending station. 
Further more it is the only station where it showed in the pair twice in the top 
ten connections. It is far from a residential suburb—it's a bustling downtown 
core combining dense residential towers with cultural venues, major transit 
options, and active pedestrian zones.

Marketing efforts should focus on the top 3–5 stations for physical promotions, 
and route-based advertisements might be most effective between frequently 
connected stations.Special attention should be given to  
Columbus Dr & Randolph St station.

### Rider Duration Distribution with a Normal Overlay

In order to showcase the distribution of ride lengths and show how they differ
based on whether they are casual riders or subscribers, the data was summarized
into the mean and standard deviations of each type and then plotted to show
their normal distribution chart. Ride lengths more than 60 minutes were
excluded in this section since there were a number of rides lasting multiple
weeks, which would make the chart impossible to scale effectively.

```{r normal, echo=FALSE}
# Compute mean and sd manually by group
dist_params <- all_trips %>%
  filter(ride_length < 60) %>%
  group_by(member_casual) %>%
  summarise(
    mu = mean(ride_length),
    sigma = sd(ride_length)
  )

# Summarize stats for labels
dist_stats <- all_trips %>%
  filter(ride_length < 60) %>%
  group_by(member_casual) %>%
  summarise(
    mu = mean(ride_length),
    sigma = sd(ride_length),
    median_val = median(ride_length),
    .groups = "drop"
  )

# Histogram and density plot
plot_dist <- all_trips %>%
  filter(ride_length < 60) %>%
  ggplot(aes(x = ride_length, fill = member_casual)) +
  
  # Histogram
  geom_histogram(aes(y = after_stat(density)), bins = 60,
                 alpha = 0.4, position = "identity") +

  # Normal curve overlays (member and casual)
  stat_function(
    fun = dnorm,
    args = list(
      mean = dist_stats$mu[dist_stats$member_casual == "member"],
      sd = dist_stats$sigma[dist_stats$member_casual == "member"]
    ),
    color = "blue", linewidth = 1.2
  ) +
  stat_function(
    fun = dnorm,
    args = list(
      mean = dist_stats$mu[dist_stats$member_casual == "casual"],
      sd = dist_stats$sigma[dist_stats$member_casual == "casual"]
    ),
    color = "red", linewidth = 1.2
  ) +

  # Add vertical lines
  geom_vline(data = dist_stats, aes(xintercept = mu), color = "darkblue", linetype = "dashed") +
  geom_vline(data = dist_stats, aes(xintercept = median_val), color = "darkgreen", linetype = "dotted") +

  # Add text labels for mean, median, and SD
  geom_text(
    data = dist_stats,
    aes(x = mu, y = 0.06, label = paste0("Mean: ", round(mu, 1))),
    angle = 90, hjust = -0.2, color = "darkblue", size = 3
  ) +
  geom_text(
    data = dist_stats,
    aes(x = median_val, y = 0.06, label = paste0("Median: ", round(median_val, 1))),
    angle = 90, hjust = -0.2, color = "darkgreen", size = 3
  ) +
  geom_text(
    data = dist_stats,
    aes(x = mu + sigma, y = 0.04,
        label = paste0("SD: ±", round(sigma, 1))),
    hjust = 0, color = "gray30", size = 3
  ) +

  facet_wrap(~member_casual) +
  labs(
    title = "Ride Duration Distribution with Mean, Median, and SD",
    subtitle = "Histogram with Normal Curve (Ride Duration < 60 min)",
    x = "Ride Length (minutes)",
    y = "Density"
  ) +
  theme_minimal(base_size = 12) +
  theme(
    strip.text = element_text(face = "bold"),
    plot.title = element_text(face = "bold", size = 14)
  )

plot_dist


```  

From the graph we see that the mean, median and standard deviations for the members
are very low compared to the casual. 
From this normal distribution showcased of each user type shown we can see that 
the casual is highly variable as we can see from the standard deviation 14.3 compared 
to 8 for the members. 

### Ride Duration by Gender and User Type (2019 only)  

The 2019 data set also included gender and age, which allows us to look into
those factors to further expand on demographics to be targeted for advertising.
In this case, we looked at the differences of ride durations based on gender
as shown in the following table:

```{r gender, echo=FALSE, message=FALSE}
df_2019 %>%
  group_by(member_casual, gender) %>%
  summarise(
    avg_duration = mean(ride_length),
    sd_duration = sd(ride_length),
    median_age = median(age),
    total_rides = n()
  ) |> 
  knitr::kable()
```

As seen with this table, there isn't a lot of variation between genders other
than the number of rides, as there are many more male users than female users.
However, there is a big difference between casual and member rides shown in
their use times and ages. This difference showcases that casual members go
on longer rides, and they tend to be younger in comparison to subscribers.
And so in order to effectively advertise to casual riders the marketing team
should focus on younger users using bikes for longer, with no gender targeting.


### Paired t-test of Ride Lengths on Weekdays vs Weekends (2019 Only)

To compare the ride lengths between members and casual users, we need to look at
the member usage to see if there really is a big difference between members
using bikes on weekends vs weekdays.

```{r ttest, echo=FALSE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE}
library(stats)
weekday <- df_2019 %>% filter(member_casual == "member", !day_of_week %in% c("Sat", "Sun"))
weekend <- df_2019 %>% filter(member_casual == "member", day_of_week %in% c("Sat", "Sun"))
test <- t.test(weekday$ride_length, weekend$ride_length, paired = FALSE)  # Welch t-test (unpaired as riders vary)
library(gginference)
ggttest(test)
```

As shown in the normal distribution of ride lengths, the t test showcases a
big difference with a t value of -2.4958, meaning there's a definite difference
between members using bikes on weekends and those using bikes on weekdays (the
closer to 0, the more similar they are). Since the t test exposes this
statistical difference between weekends and weekdays, there is an argument to
be made that members don't use bikes anywhere near as often on weekends, which
could mean there are more potential subscribers who can use bikes on weekends
with no change to bike availability, and these subscribers could be pulled
from casual users (of which 45.5% ride on weekends).


## Act

As seen with the results of the analysis, the best way to market Cyclistic's
subscription service would be to target the weekend casual riders, specifically
using the most popular stations, and advertise the convenience behind a
subscription. This could be done with several styles of posters, either on the
bikes, in the stations or even along the route between stations. Another way
to increase subscription rates would be to advertise to casual users of the
bikeshare app such as through advertisements after securing a ride.

To expand my finding, some easy data would be finding out more seasonal-based
habits and trends, as since I only looked at the first three months of 2019
and 2020 I couldn't objectively make conclusions on yearly trends from the data.

In conclusion, I recommend 
* targeting younger casual users
* targeting weekend casual riders
* targeting high traffic stations and routes

Following these three recommendations, Cyclistic will expand its subscriber
base and maximize converting casual members to subscribers.

